### They want shared rituals and memorable experiences that work for a small child.

6 of 80 posts.

These posts concern two- and three-year-olds, with older siblings also present in the cabin account. Parents and a grandparent describe or plan camping, building together on Father’s Day, seasonal cabin life, a recreated VHS movie day, Christmas traditions and a special weekly home ritual. Some are reports of joy; others ask for practical ideas or help making the activity work.

- "We babysit our 2 year old granddaughter two days weekly. I’d like to start a special routine to do with her one day a week. Something low key and not requiring leaving the house. I was thinking about the slice and bake cookies but my dtr already does that with her. Im looking for something that she’ll look forward to knowing it’s something special she does with us when she comes that day." [ar045-0065]
- "I’m still trying to envision what I want our family Christmas traditions to look like. We are away from family and don’t see any for Christmas, so for a decade, my husband and I have built our own traditions but most of them aren’t especially toddler-friendly… I’m also aware that our lives are busier than ever, so I don’t want to set big expectations that I’ll then feel I’ve let down later on." [ar072-0053]
- "Happy father’s day all! Even though my oldest is only 3 we sort of have a tradition to build something together on fathers day. Started with a box for polaroid photos, then a bird feeder outside his bedroom window, we do the home depot and lowes programs now, so he is really into building!" [ar024-0044]
- "I know she wouldn't be able to handle doing smores normally, so I feel like a genius and improvised; smores bowls with sugar free chocolate pudding cups! Needless to say, she approved and after the first night I learned to make my own since she wouldn't share 🤣" [ar021-0064]
- "[…]we got some snacks, built a little pillow fort and watched Toy Story and Cinderella on my parents living room floor just like I used to when I was her age.[…]" [ar063-0063]
- "In the winter the cabin is covered in snow. We have a thin path to walk up to the porch because I only shoveled a little bit. The fire is roaring all day long as we stay close. But we venture out to play still. We build snowmen and make snow angels. We read books and play board games. We use the telescope to look at the stars because it's not passed their bedtime when it gets dark. The kids help me fetch wood, and we keep a good supply right outside the door. At night, just before bed, I like to go alone and look back at the red glow of the fire in the dark room." [ar073-0071]

**But:** The wish for memorable traditions includes limits on expectations: "[…]I’m also aware that our lives are busier than ever, so I don’t want to set big expectations that I’ll then feel I’ve let down later on." [ar072-0053]

