### They struggle when the child expects them to direct or join play all the time.

5 of 80 posts.

These parents and one relative caregiver describe children aged two to three and a half who need adult attention or initiative to keep playing. Repeated pretend scenarios, physical clinginess, unused toys and staring at the adult when they stop are different versions of the demand. Some compare this child with a sibling or their own childhood, and one worries about the transition to school.

- "I have to play with her all day long and give her my attention 100% of the time, otherwise it’s tantrum… she has to sit on my legs or touch me or grab my hands to bring me somewhere. I am a SAHM for the moment (parental leave in my country)." [ar065-0039]
- "My LO will be 3 at the end of September, love her so much but she doesn’t have an ounce of independence in her when it comes to play. She constantly wants to play the exact same pretend scenarios over and over again. I am so tired of pretending to be Ursula or an evil step mother. When will she start playing pretend by herself with stuffed animals or Barbie’s?! I’d pop a sister out for her to play with asap if I could but in the meantime does anyone know when it gets easier?!" [ar032-0071]
- "I have always rotated toys, most all are open ended, some are not. He has a climbing tower that he doesn't mess with. I always have to initiate any play even with the toys he does like." [ar027-0093]
- "[…]I’m just wondering how to encourage more independent play, not to leave them alone or ignore them but to have them play even for 15 minutes while I sit beside them and sip a coffee .[…]" [ar046-0084]
- "But my 2-year-old? Absolutely not. He plays with cars, and trains, and he mimics things, and he wants music and he likes toys for the sake of playing with toys. And he wants us to play with him, *all the time*, which is awesome and also exhausting." [ar065-0143]

**But:** The demand coexists with pleasure in the child: "Both kids are so smart in their own way. I love them both equally. Their personalities are so different. I love the conversations with my oldest, and I love the creativity of my toddler." [ar065-0144]

